  "subtopic": "blocks",
  "title": "Statement bodies",
  "summary": "The bodies of control-flow statements and bare blocks fold",
  "prose": "Each branch of an `if`/`else` or a `try`/`catch` folds on its own: the line\nthat closes one branch and opens the next stays visible.",
  "status": "supported",
  "issues": [],
  "files": [
    {
      "path": "main.cpp",
      "text": "void process(int count) {\n    if (count > 0) {\n        for (int i = 0; i < count; i += 1) {\n            count -= 1;\n        }\n    }\n\n    while (count > 0) {\n        count -= 1;\n    }\n\n    {\n        int scratch = count;\n        count = scratch + 1;\n    }\n\n    if (count > 10) {\n        count += 1;\n    } else {\n        count -= 1;\n    }\n\n    try {\n        count *= 2;\n    } catch (...) {\n        count = 0;\n    }\n\n    do {\n        count -= 1;\n    } while (count > 10);\n}\n",
      "markers": []
    }
